evolution-jmap (0.7.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Shared mailboxes: a mailbox another user shares with you now
    appears in the folder tree under a "Shared" folder, with one
    heading per owner. Reading, searching, moving, copying, deleting,
    expunging, subscribing, creating, renaming and deleting folders,
    and saving a message into such a mailbox all go to the owner's
    account, and a message deleted there lands in the owner's Trash. A
    read-only share is shown read-only. Shared folders show even when
    their owner has not subscribed to them. The user guide lists the
    limits, including the shared read/unread flag.
  * A new Delegates page in the account editor shows who may access
    your calendars and address books, and lets you grant or revoke
    access by level (Reviewer, Author, Editor, and free/busy only for
    calendars). A delegate can be typed as an email address, or picked
    with Find on a server that offers principal search. The page is
    read-only while offline.
  * The Server Filters page became a real Sieve editor: syntax
    highlighting, a local syntax check that underlines the offending
    line and explains it in a tooltip, and word completion
    (Ctrl+Space, or automatically while typing). Activate, Deactivate,
    Rename, Add and Remove, and the script text itself once it
    validates, are now written to the server at once instead of only
    on the account editor's OK. An "Edit in External Editor" button
    opens the script in the editor configured for Evolution's External
    Editor plugin, or in $VISUAL or $EDITOR. The page is read-only
    while offline and still shows the last loaded scripts when opened
    offline. A script the server holds that this page cannot interpret
    is refused safely instead of aborting Evolution. Tab now moves
    focus out of the editor.
  * Offline mail changes are queued and replayed: a message moved or
    copied while offline shows in its new folder at once and
    disappears from the old one, survives quitting and restarting
    Evolution, and reaches the server when you are back online. Moving
    a message again before the first move has landed, copying and then
    moving, and moving a message with an unsaved flag change all queue
    correctly. A message saved while offline (for example a draft) is
    kept in a store that never expires and uploaded on reconnect. A
    queued change the server refuses for good is undone and the reason
    shown; a transport failure keeps it queued for the next attempt.
    See "Working offline" in the user guide.
  * Push notifications now use the JMAP WebSocket channel (RFC 8887)
    when the server offers push over it, and fall back to the
    EventSource channel otherwise.
  * The Vacation Responder page is read-only while offline, Tab leaves
    its message body instead of inserting a tab character, and its
    date fields work with a screen reader.
  * Snoozing a message on a server that creates the Snoozed mailbox on
    first use now refreshes the folder list so the new folder shows.
  * A tunnelled, self-hosted account's "rebase URLs" setting now also
    applies to its address books, calendars and mail, which used to
    try the real host the tunnel hides. Credentials are now also sent
    to a server configured with a plain http address on the same
    origin, such as a local tunnel.
  * Cyrus IMAP interoperability: deleting a calendar works despite
    Cyrus's non-standard argument name, push stays off because Cyrus's
    push channel blocks every other request, and a server that answers
    an anonymous request with success instead of 401 no longer
    discards a valid OAuth 2.0 refresh token.
  * The client now respects the server's limits on concurrent requests
    and uploads, and refreshes a stale session before a sync pass
    instead of working from one the server no longer recognises.
  * Mail cache fixes: "View Source" and header fetches could show a
    truncated message from a cache entry cut short by a crash, or a
    partially written one on evolution-data-server 3.52 to 3.61; both
    now fall back to the complete copy. A message whose server id
    starts with a dash was never cached; it is now. The folder-tree
    cache is written with private permissions.
  * Labels: a keyword containing a space, a percent sign or a control
    character now round-trips exactly between the server and
    Evolution's labels instead of being split or lost. The user guide
    documents which labels and categories Thunderbird, Apple Mail,
    Fastmail and Roundcube share with Evolution.
  * A mailbox whose name contains a line break can now be opened, and
    two mailboxes with empty or colliding names no longer merge into
    one folder.
  * Push and refresh robustness: push no longer silently stops for an
    account after the system refuses to start a worker thread; a
    disconnect no longer waits on a slow server's folder listing; a
    late folder-listing answer can no longer overwrite a newer one; a
    message the server refused to update but still lists comes back on
    the next refresh.
  * Calendar events with ten or more participants, unusual time zone
    names or odd whitespace no longer change on every sync round trip.
  * Hardening against hostile input: control characters in an envelope
    address, a cross-origin redirect of a POST, server ids containing
    a slash, file: and data: URIs in calendar locations and
    attachments, a carriage return in a filter rule, a bidirectional
    override in a label, and a folder list thousands of siblings wide
    are all refused, escaped or capped before they reach Evolution.
  * Fixed memory leaks in the client's session refresh and in the
    collection backend's source loading.
  * Build and packaging: GtkSourceView 4 is a new build and runtime
    dependency (for the Sieve editor); the Flatpak extension builds it
    in. The mock server gained conformance checks for RFC 8984, 9610,
    9661 and 9670 and the JMAP calendars draft (see its own
    changelog).

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 09:05:51 -0800

evolution-jmap (0.6.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a security hole in the address book preview's "Load image"
    button for a contact's externally hosted photo: a contact could
    carry a forged button that fetched a URL of the sender's choosing
    when clicked, because the button always used the same fixed,
    guessable identifier. The button now gets a fresh, unguessable one
    on every render, so a forged button cannot be reached this way.
    This affects every release from 0.6.0 to 0.6.5; upgrading is
    recommended.
  * Removed the mail view's own "Load image" placeholder for a vCard
    photo attached to a message: it had the same forgeable-button
    flaw, reachable from a plain HTML message with no vCard attachment
    at all. It was never released. Evolution's own "Load remote
    content" setting already covers the mail case, so no replacement
    was added.
  * The folder tree, and messages already read, now still show after
    starting Evolution with no network, or after restarting it
    offline.
  * Deleting a message, emptying Trash, and expunging a folder now
    succeed while offline; the change reaches the server once you
    reconnect.
  * The Vacation Responder, Server Filters, and Send Later pages now
    say "offline" right away, instead of waiting for a connection
    attempt to time out.
  * A reconnect that keeps failing no longer loops forever: Evolution
    now falls back to the cached folder tree instead.
  * "Download messages for offline use" and "Copy folder content
    locally" no longer redownload every message each time; only
    messages not already cached are fetched.
  * Fixed a message that could disappear and reappear on every refresh
    when its own id needed sanitizing.
  * Fixed marking a message Junk, and another client marking it Not
    Junk at nearly the same time, leaving both labels on the message.
  * A contact synced from the server now gets a usable "File under"
    name by default, so Evolution's contact editor can save further
    edits to it (it used to refuse to enable Save while that field
    stayed blank).
  * Fixed a calendar invitation or contact URL (ATTACH, IMAGE,
    CONFERENCE, ATTENDEE, ORGANIZER) gaining stray backslashes before
    a ';' or ','.
  * Deleting a calendar or address book now also deletes its events or
    contacts, instead of leaving them behind on the server.
  * Fixed a contact or calendar event from the server with a NUL byte
    in a field being saved as a truncated, malformed vCard or
    iCalendar document; the NUL byte is now replaced with a visible
    placeholder character instead.
  * Hardened against a malicious or buggy server: a crafted address
    can no longer forge a second, fake address in the message list by
    breaking out of its angle brackets; a stray CR or LF in a subject
    or address is now replaced instead of planting a forged extra
    line; and a bidi override character in a subject or sender name
    can no longer change the reading direction of the columns beside
    it.
  * Many small accessibility and keyboard fixes across the Server
    Filters page, its rule dialog, Vacation Responder, Send Later,
    snooze, and the undo-delete notice: fixed keyboard shortcuts that
    clashed with each other, made Enter confirm dialogs that used to
    ignore it, gave screen readers names for fields and buttons that
    had none and spoken announcements for status changes, and made
    every control reachable with the keyboard alone. Dialog spacing,
    capitalization, and ellipses now follow GNOME's own style
    consistently.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 08:17:52 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.6.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Server Filters page no longer scrolls sideways. A long error
    message or a long Sieve line now wraps instead of making the page
    wider than the window.
  * A rate-limited request now shows a plain sentence that tells you how
    long to wait. Before, it showed a raw technical message.
  * Contact groups (mailing lists) from the server now show their name
    and members correctly in the Contact List Editor.
  * Fixed a contact id that could clash with the server's own id on some
    servers, including Stalwart 1.0. The clash could make a new contact
    invisible to a group or to later changes.
  * Fixed a new contact or calendar event that could stay invisible until
    a full resync, when another program on the same account made a
    change at nearly the same time.
  * Saving the Vacation Responder, a Server Filters script, a contact, or
    a calendar event no longer risks silently overwriting a change
    another program made at the same time. The save now detects the
    conflict and handles it the normal way.
  * Fixed two fast clicks on a snooze option, or on Send Later, sending
    the action twice.
  * Fixed a crash that could happen when the composer, the Server
    Filters page, or the Undo notice was closed while one of its own
    dialogs was still open.
  * Fixed a fetched contact photo that could briefly show in the wrong
    contact.
  * Hardened the JMAP client against a hostile or broken server. It now
    rejects a response it did not ask for, a duplicate or out-of-order
    response, a replayed response, and a session state that goes
    backward. It no longer sends your credentials to a server response
    that redirects to another origin.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 06:15:36 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.6.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Added a Server Filters page to the account editor for servers that
    support JMAP Sieve filters. It lists and manages filter scripts, and
    offers a rules list for one managed script so most users never need
    to write Sieve by hand. If another program changes the filters while
    the page is open, the page does not overwrite that change.
  * This release replaces 0.6.0, whose release build failed before
    publishing. See the v0.6.1 tag message for detail.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:53:46 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Calendars and address books that another user shares are now shown as
    additional collections, so they can be browsed and, with the right
    permissions, edited. Deleting a shared calendar or address book now
    acts on the correct account and is only offered when the user
    actually has permission to remove it.
  * An address book or calendar without write access now opens read-only,
    so Evolution greys out editing instead of letting a save fail
    afterwards.
  * A shared calendar or address book's display name can no longer be set
    by its owner to hide who shared it: the name is now always "owner:
    collection name".
  * The server now sends meeting invitations, updates and cancellations
    itself. A clear error is shown when the server cannot do this for one
    of the guests, and Evolution's own "do not send invitation" option
    still suppresses a single change. A plain import or backup restore of
    a meeting no longer mails its organizer by mistake, and neither does
    removing an invitation that was already cancelled.
  * Dismissing a calendar reminder is now sent to the server, so the same
    reminder does not fire again on your other devices.
  * Editing a contact or event that was changed elsewhere at the same
    time now follows Evolution's own conflict choice (keep mine, keep the
    server's, or fail) instead of silently overwriting the other change.
  * An "Undo" notice now appears after deleting a calendar or address
    book collection.
  * Contact photos are now uploaded and downloaded through the server's
    blob storage, handling larger images without encoding them inline.
    You are told when a photo is too large for the server to store.
  * Added a "Load image" option for an externally hosted (http or https)
    contact photo in the address book preview: a placeholder and the URL
    are shown instead of loading the image automatically, and the photo
    is fetched only on request.
  * Snooze is now reachable by right-clicking a message's body, not only
    through its menu button, including in a detached message window.
    Fixed the "Next Monday Morning" snooze preset landing on the wrong
    day.
  * Fixed newly created mail folders becoming invisible on servers that
    default new folders to unsubscribed.
  * Fixed a deadlock that could freeze Evolution while an address book
    refreshed in the background.
  * Fixed a pushed folder change (for example, creating the Snoozed
    folder) sometimes jumping the mail view to a different account.
  * Deleting a message now moves it to Trash as usual; it is only
    permanently removed when Trash is emptied.
  * Fixed Send Later sometimes choosing the wrong outgoing identity.
  * Large batches of changes that exceed a server's per-request limit are
    now split automatically instead of failing outright.
  * Server rate-limit responses are now retried automatically with a
    backoff, without duplicating any writes.
  * The JMAP client now refuses a server redirect that would drop the
    connection from https to http, so credentials are never sent over a
    plain connection.
  * Updated the bundled TLS library (rustls-webpki, webpki-roots) to
    close two published security advisories (RUSTSEC-2026-0381 and
    RUSTSEC-2026-0382).
  * Updated the German translation (fully translated, no missing or
    fuzzy strings).

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 07:49:38 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a brand-new account silently signing in twice on its first
    connect, caused by its own setup accidentally triggering a second
    sign-in.
  * Editing a JMAP account's server address now triggers a fresh sign-in
    instead of needing Evolution restarted to take effect.
  * Fixed an endless chain of sign-in prompts when an account's server
    address ends in a trailing dot (for example api.fastmail.com.): the
    dot names the same server and is now treated that way, credentials
    survive the server's redirect, and a redirect that cannot carry them
    is reported as a clear connection error instead of another consent
    window.
  * A redirect to a malformed address from the server is now reported as
    an error instead of crashing the connection attempt.
  * A new account's Sent and Drafts folders are now configured
    automatically from the server's own folder roles, the same way
    Inbox, Trash and Junk already were.
  * Searching a folder's message bodies is now answered by the server, on
    evolution-data-server 3.58 and newer, instead of silently finding
    nothing.
  * An account whose saved sync state the server no longer recognises
    (for example after the server was restored from backup) now
    resynchronises automatically instead of getting stuck until its local
    cache is deleted by hand.
  * On evolution-data-server 3.62 and newer, the mail cache is written
    atomically, protecting it against corruption if Evolution is
    interrupted mid-write.
  * On evolution-data-server 3.63.1 and newer, OAuth 2.0 account setup
    uses evolution-data-server's own OAuth2Dynamic support instead of
    this project's own implementation. There is no migration: an
    existing account using the old method needs to be set up again after
    upgrading to such a build.
  * A round of interoperability fixes against real JMAP servers (tested
    against Stalwart): folder creation, calendar participants and
    notifications, and shared-resource ownership now tolerate several
    real-server quirks that used to produce spurious errors.
  * Setting up or reconnecting several JMAP accounts at once no longer
    lets one slow or unreachable server's address lookup delay the
    others.
  * Added a German translation.
  * Updated the bundled TLS library (rustls) to close four published
    security advisories (RUSTSEC-2026-0346 through RUSTSEC-2026-0349).
  * Mail, contacts and calendars have all been verified against a real
    JMAP server at real-world scale (up to 10,000 messages, 5,000
    contacts and 3,000 calendar events, including through
    evolution-data-server itself), with no correctness problems found.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:52:07 -0700

evolution-jmap (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rebuild of 0.4.0 through the repaired release pipeline; no code changes.
    The container image the pipeline builds the .deb in was orphaned when
    the repository was re-created, so 0.4.0's release workflow could not
    run and its .deb was uploaded by hand, without the pipeline's Sigstore
    provenance. The image is now published as this repository's own
    ci-image package and the workflow pins its digest; 0.4.1's artifacts
    are pipeline-built and attested again.

